      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e \u003e Are you suggesting to ignore any node with device_type?\n\n\u003e No no! I meant that you can detect CPUs by parsing \u003cdevice_type\u003e in the loop: if it\u0027s a CPU, you treat it in a special way, otherwise you ignore the property.\n\u003e I guess that ignoring those nodes would be fine as another part of the code is already validating their \u003creg\u003e and they can\u0027t clash with assigned devices.\n\nI thought that the bug is filed to validate whether the `\u003creg\u003e` of assigned device nodes.\n\nCould you be more specific about \u0027ignoring those nodes\u0027  part? Are you referring to `device_type \u003d cpu` only or any nodes with `\u003cdevice_type\u003e` prop?\n\n-----\n\n\u003e \u003e Or should I group nodes with device_type and compare within the group?\n\n\u003e That would be too simple 😄\n\u003e \n\u003e From platform.dts alone: devices with device_type may be in their own \"\u003creg\u003e space\" (\"cpu\"), be \"normal\" by occupying \u003creg\u003e ranges on the main bus (\"memory\") but also more than just \u003creg\u003e (\"pci\" has \u003cranges\u003e) so \u003cdevice_type\u003e doesn\u0027t define a \"bus\", unfortunately.\n\nYes. The solution is very naive. But we would only update `platform.dts` and `pvmfw` as a package, so I think that we only need check enough for `platform.dts`. What do you think? (things may be more complicated if an assigned device node has `device_type`, though.",
